The most frequent errors are:
mixing units, especially moles and mass, or kJ and kWh;
forgetting a stream, so the balance does not close;
assuming the equilibrium conversion is the conversion actually achieved;
ignoring by-products, heat effects and recycle streams;
memorising process descriptions without understanding why each step exists;
treating software output as correct because it looks precise.

Safety and waste are also commonly pushed aside as exam extras. They are not. A process that cannot be operated safely, or whose waste cannot be disposed of lawfully, is not a viable process, and examiners mark it that way.
